ed to pass through exactly one point in each column. That is possible if we can find a set of n - v lines that each intersect each of the columns v+1,...,n at a distinct point, covering all points in those columns. But we also need to cover the points in column v+1,...,n. The total number of points in those columns is sum_{a=v+1}^{n} (n+1-a) = sum_{t=1}^{n-v} t = (n-v)(n-v+1)/2. We have n - v lines, each can cover at most n - v points (one per column). So total capacity is (n - v)^2. Since (n - v)^2 ≥ (n - v)(n - v + 1)/2 for n - v ≥ 1, we have enough capacity. So the covering is possible in principle.\n\nThus the minimal v needed is such that v ≥ 0? Actually we need to include columns 1,...,v as vertical lines. So v can be any integer from 0 to n. Then we have n - v non-vertical lines. The condition that each column left has at most n - v points is satisfied if v ≥ 0 (since the largest column left is column v+1 with n - v points). So any v works? Let's test v=0: no vertical lines, all n lines are non-vertical. Then we have n lines, each can intersect each column at most once. The largest column (column 1) has n points, but we have only n lines, each can cover at most one point in column 1, so we can cover at most n points in column 1, which is exactly n. So column 1 can be covered. Column 2 has n-1 points, we have n lines, each can cover at most one point in column 2, so we can cover at most n points, which is enough. So in principle, we could cover all points with n non-vertical lines, each line covering one point in each column. But we need to ensure that each line can pass through exactly one point in each column, and that the set of points covered across columns for each line is consistent (i.e., the points lie on a line). This is a nontrivial combinatorial geometry problem: can we find n lines (none vertical, horizontal, or slope -1) that pass through exactly one point in each column and collectively cover all points? This is like a Latin square or a transvers